; expect 3
; A macro name is not a keyword. A binding of the same name wins, and
; both kinds of binding are checked because the old expander lost to
; neither - it lost to both.
;
; Expansion used to run at EMIT time, and `emitVar`/`dispatchCall` each
; consulted the macro table BEFORE the symbol table, the constructor
; registry and the parameter list. So a macro named `v` rewrote every
; occurrence of the identifier `v` anywhere in the program: a function
; parameter named `v` evaluated to the macro's template, and so did a
; `let` binding. Measured at 9 for each, where the program says 1 and 2.
;
; Expansion now runs before the checker, over a walk that carries the
; set of names bound at each point, so a bound name is a variable.
;
; 1 + 2 = 3; under the old rule, 9 + 9 = 18.
(macro (v) 9)

(:: f (-> Int Int))

(fn (f v) v)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (+
    (f 1)    (let ((v 2))
      v
    )
  )
)
